"device usage" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		Device
		<header>A device draws a phone, tablet or desktop round what it holds, at that device's screen size, with buttons in its thick bottom edge.</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Put what the device shows inside it, and its buttons in `slot="buttons"`:

			- `device`:  `phone` (390 by 844), `tablet` (820 by 1180) or `desktop` (1280 by 800).
			- `orientation`:  `portrait` or `landscape`;  a desktop is always wide.
			- What it holds is laid out at that size, then scaled down to fit the width it's given and the
			  window's height, never up.  Its frame and buttons stay their real size.
			- `controls`:  its own buttons, `all` (the default), `rotate`, `device` or `none`.
			- A person's Rotate or switch writes `device` / `orientation`, then sends `ui-changed`
			  (`detail.device`, `detail.orientation`).  A page's own change sends nothing.
			- `modal`:  shown in a basic modal of its own, starting hidden.  Show it with `visible`, or a button's
			  `commandfor` and `command="--show"`;  `ui-opened` and `ui-closed` say when.
			  Escape, the dimmer and a `cancel` / `deny` button in its edge hide it;  `closedby` says which.

			In Spell, `<device "phone" modal>`;  in Spanish, `<dispositivo "teléfono" modal>`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const device = document.querySelector("ui-device")
			device.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.device, event.detail.orientation))
			device.visible = true // a modal device:  show it
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			Its buttons are buttons:  Tab to them, Enter or Space to press.  As a modal, Escape hides it, and focus
			stays inside it while it shows, as in any `<ui-modal>`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The switch is a group named "Device";  each of its buttons is named for its device, the one shown
			  selected.
			- Rotate is named "Rotate".
			- A modal device takes its name from its own `aria-label`:  give it one.
		</>
	</>
